{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01D18WWVtvSHTgSDjYQYALBs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Jx1jirUJ8DukLEsTetRwWZ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** performing a blind peer review of a submitted ML paper. Your focus areas are **methodology and experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Do Reasoning LLMs Need Recovery-Aware Pruning? A Controlled Empirical Study\"\n\nThe paper proposes RASP (Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning), which scores structures as S(h) = I(h) × (1−F(h))^α, where I(h) is Wanda importance and F(h) is CKA functional overlap. It then tests this hypothesis thoroughly and reports largely negative results: CKA doesn't predict recoverability, Standard+SFT beats RASP+SFT (63.5% vs 55.3% on GSM8K), GRPO adds nothing over SFT (p=0.62). One positive finding: α-scaling improves post-prune accuracy (+12pp at α=4) but this vanishes after SFT recovery.\n\n## Paper Details (from the 10-page PDF)\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- RASP scoring: S(h) = I(h) × (1−F(h))^α\n- F(h) = mean pairwise CKA of structure h with others in same layer, computed over 512 calibration samples\n- Baselines: Standard/Wanda (α=0), GRASPrune (ACL 2026), Bonsai (ICML 2025), Unstructured magnitude, Random\n\n**Experimental Setup (Section 4):**\n- Model: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B\n- Pruning: Structured MLP channel groups at 20%, 25%, 30% sparsity (all pruned structures are MLP groups, 0/112 attention GQA groups pruned)\n- Recovery: SFT (1870 steps on GSM8K training solutions), SFT→GRPO\n- Eval: GSM8K N=1319, MATH-500, HumanEval. 5 seeds, 95% bootstrap CIs\n- Stats: Paired t-tests, Wilcoxon, 2×2 ANOVA, mixed-effects regression\n\n**Key Results Tables:**\n- Table 1: Post-prune RASP-20 29.3% vs Standard-20 28.1%, Δ=+1.2pp, p=0.34\n- Table 2: 5-seed post-prune, mean gap 0.9±0.6pp, paired t p=0.15, Cohen's d=0.35\n- Table 3: SFT recovery — RASP 55.3%, Standard 63.5% (Standard +8.2pp better)\n- Table 4: GRPO — RASP 57.8% (+2.5pp), Standard 62.1% (−1.4pp)\n- Table 5: Full pipeline — Standard+SFT best at 63.5%, recovery rate 73%\n- Table 6: 2×2 ANOVA — pruning method p=0.03, recovery method p=0.62, interaction p=0.41\n- Table 7: 4 methods full pipeline — GRASPrune 7.2%→56%, Bonsai 25%→59%, Standard 28.1%→63.5%\n- Table 8: Scoring ablation — Random 14%, Magnitude 27%, Standard 28%, RASP α=1 30%, RASP α=4 40%\n- Table 9: α sweep — post-prune monotonic increase, post-SFT non-monotonic (peaks at α=0)\n- Table 10: Mixed-effects regression — CKA β=0.08 p=0.12 R²=0.018; Gradient β=0.31 p<0.001 R²=0.14\n- Table 11: 25% sparsity same pattern; 30% near-collapse (5%/4%)\n- Table 12: OOD — Standard+SFT: MATH 30%, HumanEval 37%; RASP+SFT: 25%, 31%\n- Table 13: Unstructured 20% post-prune 54.5%, post-recovery 63.5%, speedup 1.02×\n- Table 14: Inference — RASP 25.2ms/tok, Standard 25.0ms/tok, Dense 28.5ms/tok\n\n**Discussion:**\n- CKA measures local functional similarity but recovery is global optimization\n- RASP protects functionally unique structures that may be less SFT-receptive\n- α-scaling optimizes a proxy (post-prune) that diverges from true target (post-recovery)\n- Limitations: single model, sparsity ≤30%, fixed SFT/GRPO hyperparams, math reasoning only\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full imagined experiment list contains 18 experiments:\n1. Full GSM8K Eval N=1319 (Post-Prune) — ✓ in paper (Table 1)\n2. SFT Warmup Recovery — ✓ (Table 3)\n3. GRPO Stage-2 Recovery — ✓ (Table 4)\n4. Full GSM8K Eval N=1319 (All Recovered Models) — ✓ (Table 5)\n5. GRASPrune Baseline 20% — ✓ (Table 7)\n6. Multi-Seed Post-Prune Eval (5 Seeds) — ✓ (Table 2)\n7. Scoring Ablation at 20% Sparsity — ✓ (Table 8)\n8. CKA-Recoverability Correlation Analysis — ✓ (Table 10)\n9. SFT-Only Recovery Comparison / 2×2 ANOVA — ✓ (Table 6)\n10. 25% Sparsity RASP + Standard — ✓ (Table 11)\n11. 30% Sparsity Probe — ✓ (Table 11)\n12. OOD Evaluation (MATH-500, HumanEval) — ✓ (Table 12)\n13. Bonsai Baseline 20% — ✓ (Table 7)\n14. CKA Reliability Analysis — ✓ (mentioned in Section 5.8 text)\n15. α Sensitivity Ablation — ✓ (Table 9)\n16. Inference Speedup Benchmark — ✓ (Table 14)\n17. Unstructured Pruning Baseline 20% — ✓ (Table 13)\n18. SFT OOD Eval Contingency — ✓ (merged into Table 12)\n\nAll 18 planned experiments are represented.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### 1. Methodology Soundness\n- Is the RASP formulation well-motivated and clearly defined?\n- Is the CKA functional overlap computation valid (mean pairwise intra-layer CKA)?\n- Are the baselines appropriate and fairly compared?\n- Are there methodological flaws or confounds?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Statistical analysis quality (5-seed, paired tests, ANOVA, mixed-effects regression)\n- Sample sizes and power (N=1319 for GSM8K, N=5 seeds)\n- Confidence intervals and effect sizes\n- Are the conclusions supported by the evidence?\n- Coverage: all 18 planned experiments present?\n\n### 3. Specific Concerns\n- The scoring ablation (Table 8) uses N=100, not N=1319 — is this a concern?\n- Only MLP groups are pruned (0 attention groups) — does this limit CKA relevance?\n- Single model (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B) — how much does this limit generalizability?\n- The α=4 confound: is the 12pp gain from CKA or from importance distribution reshaping? Paper acknowledges this but doesn't test I(h)^α without CKA.\n- SFT uses GSM8K training data and primary eval is GSM8K — train/test contamination concern?\n- GRPO hyperparameters fixed — could alternative GRPO configs change the null result?\n\n### 4. Missing Experiments or Analyses\n- Any critical experiments missing from the paper?\n- Would additional analyses strengthen or weaken the claims?\n\n### 5. Strengths (be specific)\n\n### 6. Weaknesses (be specific, ranked by severity)\n\n### 7. Questions for Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n\n### 8. Score\nRate on a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\nTarget venue: top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate).\n\nProvide: Overall score, Confidence (1-5), and a one-line summary recommendation.\n\nFormat your review clearly with headers. Be thorough but concise. Focus on what matters most for a methods+experiments reviewer."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":40360,"cache_read_input_tokens":22962,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":40360},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"338b77c3-ab77-4d20-989d-1a060abc37a8","uuid":"a7bfd93b-ce34-400b-8e93-565c547d450b"}
