{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kqy81iHp6ucc5JEb7moiUi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\\label{sec:intro}\n3\t\n4\tLarge language models trained with Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) achieve strong performance on mathematical and logical reasoning~\\citep{deepseek2025r1,shao2024grpo}, but their parameter counts and extended chain-of-thought traces make deployment costly.\n5\tStructured pruning---removing entire attention heads or MLP channels---offers a hardware-friendly compression path that reduces both memory and latency without specialized sparse-computation support~\\citep{llmpruner2023,graspprune2026}.\n6\tHowever, structured pruning at moderate sparsity severely degrades reasoning ability, necessitating a post-pruning recovery stage.\n7\t\\citet{selfhealing2025} show that RLVR-based recovery substantially outperforms SFT-based recovery for pruned reasoning models, establishing RLVR as the preferred recovery paradigm.\n8\t\n9\tDespite this, current approaches treat pruning and recovery as independent steps: pruning methods optimize for immediate post-pruning accuracy~\\citep{resp2025,graspprune2026,bonsai2025}, while recovery methods operate on whatever architecture they receive~\\citep{selfhealing2025,paser2025}.\n10\tNo prior work has studied whether the \\emph{choice} of what to prune affects how well RLVR can subsequently recover reasoning---or whether this interaction differs from SFT-based recovery.\n11\tThis gap is consequential because RLVR and SFT impose fundamentally different functional demands on the retained architecture: \\citet{rlsqueezes2026} show that RLVR recovers through global exploration and path compression, while SFT recovers through local trajectory imitation.\n12\tThe optimal set of structures to retain should therefore depend on the recovery paradigm.\n13\t\n14\tWe hypothesize that \\emph{functional overlap}---the degree to which a structure's computation is redundant with its within-layer peers---positively predicts RLVR recoverability.\n15\tIf a pruned structure has high overlap with retained structures, RLVR can absorb its function through global policy optimization over the remaining architecture.\n16\tConversely, structures with unique, non-redundant computations are difficult to recover once removed.\n17\tThis yields what we term the \\textbf{Redundancy Paradox}: counterintuitively, the structures most valuable to retain for post-RLVR recovery are not the most important ones, but the most \\emph{functionally unique} ones.\n18\tRecovery-aware pruning therefore \\emph{preferentially removes} important-but-redundant structures---whose high overlap means RLVR can redistribute their function across retained peers---while protecting functionally unique structures that, once removed, leave an irrecoverable gap.\n19\tStandard importance-only pruning, by contrast, protects high-importance structures regardless of redundancy, inadvertently removing unique structures whose function cannot be recovered.\n20\t\n21\tWe propose \\textbf{Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning (\\rasp{})}, a three-stage framework that incorporates recoverability into the pruning decision.\n22\tIn Stage~1, \\rasp{} computes for each structure both a gradient-based importance score and a functional overlap score via intra-layer Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA;~\\citealp{kornblith2019cka}).\n23\tIn Stage~2, these are combined into a recovery-aware score $S(h) = I(h) \\times (1 - F(h))^\\alpha$ that protects structures which are both important \\emph{and} functionally unique, while allowing removal of important-but-redundant structures whose function RLVR can redistribute.\n24\tIn Stage~3, the pruned model undergoes a brief SFT warmup to restore generation competence, followed by GRPO-based RLVR recovery~\\citep{shao2024grpo} to restore reasoning ability.\n25\tUnlike prior CKA-based pruning that uses inter-layer similarity for depth pruning~\\citep{mpruner2024,lachance2024cka}, \\rasp{} uses \\emph{intra-layer} CKA between individual GQA groups and MLP channels to predict per-structure recoverability---a finer granularity optimizing for a fundamentally different objective.\n26\t\n27\tWe evaluate \\rasp{} on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B~\\citep{deepseek2025r1} at 20\\% structured sparsity.\n28\tAt matched sparsity, \\rasp{}-pruned models retain 29\\% GSM8K~\\citep{cobbe2021gsm8k} accuracy immediately after pruning, compared to 1\\% for importance-only pruning ($N{=}100$, $p{<}0.0001$)---a 28-percentage-point advantage attributable to the recovery-aware scoring criterion.\n29\tAfter SFT warmup, both pruning strategies recover substantially (57--64\\%), with detailed comparison deferred to \\S\\ref{sec:experiments}.\n30\t[TBD: GRPO results]\n31\t\n32\tOur contributions are:\n33\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n34\t    \\item We identify the \\emph{Redundancy Paradox}---that functional redundancy enables RLVR to recover the function of removed structures, making important-but-redundant structures safely removable while functionally unique structures become critical to retain---and formalize it through intra-layer CKA-based functional overlap scoring.\n35\t    \\item We propose \\rasp{}, a recovery-aware pruning framework that integrates functional overlap into structural pruning decisions, achieving 29\\% post-prune GSM8K accuracy versus 1\\% for importance-only pruning at matched 20\\% sparsity on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B.\n36\t    \\item [TBD: GRPO recovery results---contribution demonstrating that \\rasp{}'s post-prune advantage translates to superior post-RLVR reasoning performance.]\n37\t    \\item We present preliminary evidence that the optimal pruning strategy may be conditioned on the recovery paradigm~\\citep{frankle2019lth}, supporting the notion of \\emph{paradigm-conditioned prunability} [TBD: pending GRPO confirmation and statistical validation].\n38\t\\end{enumerate}\n39\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jx1jirUJ8DukLEsTetRwWZ","session_id":"338b77c3-ab77-4d20-989d-1a060abc37a8","uuid":"070d6be1-fb90-4473-a109-b2a3e05acc00","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:06:32.885Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01D18WWVtvSHTgSDjYQYALBs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014vLHgt2xRFWF1UkYk4SRSq","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 2 (R2)** performing a blind peer review of a submitted ML paper. Your focus areas are **novelty, significance, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Do Reasoning LLMs Need Recovery-Aware Pruning? A Controlled Empirical Study\"\n\nThe paper proposes RASP (Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning), which scores structures as S(h) = I(h) × (1−F(h))^α, where I(h) is Wanda importance and F(h) is CKA functional overlap. It then tests this hypothesis thoroughly and reports largely negative results: CKA doesn't predict recoverability, Standard+SFT beats RASP+SFT (63.5% vs 55.3% on GSM8K), GRPO adds nothing over SFT (p=0.62). One positive finding: α-scaling improves post-prune accuracy (+12pp at α=4) but this vanishes after SFT recovery.\n\n## Paper Details (from the 10-page PDF)\n\n**Introduction claims:**\n1. CKA overlap does not predict recoverability (β=0.08, p=0.12, incr. R²=1.8%)\n2. Standard pruning + SFT is Pareto-optimal (63.5% vs 55.3% for RASP+SFT)\n3. GRPO adds no value over SFT (p=0.62, no interaction p=0.41)\n4. α-scaling improves post-prune but not post-recovery (12pp gain vanishes after SFT)\n\n**Related Work (Section 2):**\n- Structured Pruning: LLM-Pruner (Ma et al. 2024/NeurIPS 2023), SliceGPT (Ashkboos et al. 2024/ICLR 2024), Wanda (Sun et al. 2024/ICLR 2024), GRASPrune (ACL 2026), Bonsai (ICML 2025)\n- Recovery: Self-Healing (RLVR recovery to 95%+ at 25%), PASER (optimizes recovery data), RESP (reasoning-trace calibration, no recovery)\n- CKA: Kornblith et al. 2019, MPruner (inter-layer CKA for depth pruning in CNNs)\n- Paper claims: \"The interaction between pruning structure and recovery method remains unexplored\"\n\n**Method:**\n- RASP: S(h) = I(h) × (1−F(h))^α with CKA-based F(h)\n- Model: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B\n- 5 seeds, 3 sparsity levels (20/25/30%), 4 pruning methods, 2 recovery paradigms\n- GSM8K N=1319, OOD on MATH-500 and HumanEval\n\n**Key Results:**\n- All 4 core claims supported by extensive experiments\n- 14 tables covering post-prune, SFT recovery, GRPO, full pipeline, ANOVA, baselines, scoring ablation, α sweep, CKA regression, sparsity scaling, OOD, unstructured comparison, inference benchmarks\n- Experiment coverage: all 18 planned experiments from the design document are represented\n\n**Discussion:**\n- CKA measures local similarity but recovery is global optimization\n- RASP protects functionally unique but SFT-unfriendly structures\n- α-scaling optimizes proxy (post-prune) not true target (post-recovery)\n- Practical takeaways: use Standard+SFT, skip GRPO, focus on sparsity ratio\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:**\n- Single base model\n- Sparsity ≤30%\n- Fixed SFT/GRPO hyperparameters\n- Math reasoning only\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is the RASP formulation itself?\n- How novel is the negative finding that CKA doesn't predict recoverability?\n- Is \"negative result as contribution\" sufficiently compelling for a top venue?\n- Does the α-scaling finding constitute sufficient positive contribution?\n- Is the paper more of a \"well-executed null result\" or does it provide new insight?\n\n### 2. Significance and Impact\n- Who benefits from these findings? How much?\n- Does the paper change how practitioners should approach pruning+recovery?\n- Is the finding \"SFT is robust enough to compensate for any pruning strategy\" surprising enough?\n- How does this compare to Self-Healing Authors (2025) which already showed RLVR recovers standard-pruned models to 95%+?\n\n### 3. Related Work Completeness\n- Are there missing related works that would change the paper's positioning?\n- Is the claim \"interaction between pruning structure and recovery method remains unexplored\" accurate?\n- How does this relate to knowledge distillation literature where similar \"recovery dominates structure\" findings exist?\n- Are there other CKA-for-pruning works beyond MPruner?\n- Are there other recovery-aware pruning proposals in the literature?\n\n### 4. Framing and Presentation\n- Is the paper well-framed as a negative result?\n- Does the title accurately represent the content?\n- Is the narrative coherent (testing a hypothesis → finding it false → extracting positive lessons)?\n- Are the positive takeaways (α-scaling, SFT robustness) given appropriate weight?\n\n### 5. Positioning Concerns\n- The paper tests its own proposed method (RASP) and finds it doesn't work — is this a concern?\n- Could RASP be seen as a strawman (simple CKA averaging, single formulation)?\n- Would alternative functional overlap measures (e.g., OPD, SVCCA, RSA) potentially work where CKA fails?\n- Is \"moderate sparsity\" (20-30%) the right regime to test recovery-awareness? Higher sparsity might show different results.\n\n### 6. Strengths (be specific)\n\n### 7. Weaknesses (be specific, ranked by severity)\n\n### 8. Questions for Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n\n### 9. Score\nRate on a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\nTarget venue: top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate).\n\nProvide: Overall score, Confidence (1-5), and a one-line summary recommendation.\n\nFormat your review clearly with headers. Be thorough but concise. Focus on what matters most for a novelty+related-work reviewer."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":40360,"cache_read_input_tokens":22962,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":40360},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"338b77c3-ab77-4d20-989d-1a060abc37a8","uuid":"0623af0a-a3c8-4c3c-9696-aa284644bbea"}
