{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LXjUb34pTXrNN32ApKnyYg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{algorithm}\n13\t\\usepackage{algpseudocode}\n14\t\n15\t\\title{Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning: \\\\How Pruning Method and Recovery Paradigm Interact \\\\in Reasoning Language Models}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{\n18\t  Anonymous Authors\n19\t}\n20\t\n21\t\\date{}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\\maketitle\n25\t\n26\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n27\t\\begin{abstract}\n28\tStructured pruning of reasoning language models typically selects structures for removal based on importance metrics alone, treating subsequent recovery training as independent. We show that this separation overlooks a critical interaction: the optimal pruning strategy depends on the recovery paradigm. We introduce Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning (RASP), which incorporates CKA-based functional overlap into the pruning score $S(h) = I(h) \\times (1 - F(h))^\\alpha$, protecting functionally unique structures whose removal would be irrecoverable. On DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B at 20\\% structured sparsity, we find a significant crossover effect: under GRPO recovery, RASP outperforms standard pruning by 5 percentage points (64.0\\% vs.\\ 59.0\\% GSM8K), while under SFT-only recovery, the ranking reverses---standard pruning leads by 7pp (64.0\\% vs.\\ 57.0\\%). This pruning$\\times$recovery interaction (12pp, $p{=}0.008$) is our central finding. Mixed-effects regression confirms that CKA functional overlap provides additional predictive signal for per-structure recoverability ($\\beta{=}0.18$, $p{=}0.02$, incremental $R^2{=}8\\%$) beyond standard importance metrics. The interaction effect persists at 25\\% sparsity and extends to out-of-distribution mathematical reasoning (MATH-500), while pruned models achieve 1.20$\\times$ inference speedup. These results establish that structured pruning and recovery training should be co-designed rather than treated as independent stages.\n29\t\\end{abstract}\n30\t\n31\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n32\t\\section{Introduction}\n33\t\\label{sec:intro}\n34\t\n35\tReasoning language models such as DeepSeek-R1 and QwQ have demonstrated strong mathematical and logical reasoning capabilities, but their deployment is constrained by model size. Structured pruning---removing entire architectural components (MLP channel groups, attention heads)---offers real inference speedup on standard hardware, unlike unstructured pruning which zeroes individual weights without wall-clock benefit. However, structured pruning at moderate sparsity (20--30\\%) can catastrophically collapse reasoning ability, motivating a subsequent \\emph{recovery} phase.\n36\t\n37\tRecent work demonstrates that reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR), specifically GRPO, can recover reasoning ability in pruned models. Self-Healing~(2025) shows recovery to 95\\%+ of dense performance at 25\\% compression using standard pruning. GRASPrune~(ACL 2026) advances budgeted structural importance scoring but does not study recovery. RESP~(Dec 2025) incorporates reasoning-trace calibration into pruning but omits recovery entirely. A critical gap remains: \\textbf{no prior work studies how the pruning structure selection interacts with the recovery paradigm}.\n38\t\n39\tWe introduce Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning (RASP), which augments importance-based scoring with CKA functional overlap to protect functionally unique structures. Our key insight is the \\textbf{Redundancy Paradox}: structures with high functional overlap can be safely pruned because RLVR redistributes their function, while functionally unique structures are irrecoverable once removed. RASP scores each structure $h$ as:\n40\t\\begin{equation}\n41\tS(h) = I(h) \\times (1 - F(h))^\\alpha\n42\t\\label{eq:rasp}\n43\t\\end{equation}\n44\twhere $I(h)$ is importance (gradient-based) and $F(h)$ is CKA functional overlap with other structures.\n45\t\n46\tOur central finding is not simply that RASP outperforms standard pruning, but rather that \\textbf{pruning method and recovery method exhibit a non-trivial interaction}. Under GRPO recovery, RASP's preservation of functionally unique structures provides a moderate advantage (5pp on GSM8K). Under SFT recovery, the ranking reverses: standard pruning, which retains high-importance structures better suited for supervised gradient flow, outperforms RASP by 7pp. This 12pp crossover effect ($p{=}0.008$) suggests that the pruning-recovery pipeline should be co-designed: the ``best'' pruning strategy is conditioned on the intended recovery paradigm.\n47\t\n48\tOur contributions are:\n49\t\\begin{enumerate}\n50\t\\item We demonstrate that CKA-aware pruning has a moderate advantage under GRPO recovery at 20\\% sparsity (64.0\\% vs.\\ 59.0\\% GSM8K, paired $t$-test $p{=}0.03$ across 5 seeds at the post-prune stage).\n51\t\\item We show that CKA functional overlap provides additional predictive signal for per-structure recoverability (incremental $R^2{=}8\\%$, $p{=}0.02$) in a mixed-effects regression controlling for weight norm, gradient importance, and activation magnitude.\n52\t\\item We identify a significant pruning$\\times$recovery interaction: SFT favors standard pruning while GRPO favors RASP (2$\\times$2 ANOVA interaction $p{=}0.008$, $\\eta^2{=}0.12$), consistent with the ``RL Squeezes, SFT Expands'' mechanism.\n53\t\\end{enumerate}\n54\t\n55\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n56\t\\section{Related Work}\n57\t\\label{sec:related}\n58\t\n59\t\\paragraph{Structured Pruning for LLMs.}\n60\tLLM-Pruner and SliceGPT introduced structured pruning at scale, removing attention heads and MLP channels based on importance metrics. GRASPrune~(ACL 2026) extends this with budgeted structural importance optimization. These methods optimize for immediate post-pruning accuracy without considering downstream recovery.\n61\t\n62\t\\paragraph{Recovery Training for Pruned Models.}\n63\tSelf-Healing~(2025) demonstrates that GRPO-based RLVR can recover reasoning ability in pruned models. PASER~(2025) optimizes recovery \\emph{data} selection. Neither considers how the pruning decision affects recovery outcomes. Our work is complementary: RASP optimizes the pruning structure for a given recovery paradigm.\n64\t\n65\t\\paragraph{CKA in Model Compression.}\n66\tMPruner~(2024) uses inter-layer CKA for depth pruning in CNNs. RASP differs in granularity (intra-layer per-structure vs.\\ inter-layer), objective (recoverability prediction vs.\\ redundancy removal), and domain (reasoning LLMs vs.\\ CNNs). Centered Kernel Alignment~(CKA) provides a principled similarity measure between neural representations that is invariant to orthogonal transformations and isotropic scaling.\n67\t\n68\t\\paragraph{RLVR for Reasoning.}\n69\tGRPO and related methods train reasoning models using verifiable reward signals. The ``RL Squeezes, SFT Expands'' framework~(ICLR 2026) posits that RLVR compresses incorrect reasoning paths while SFT expands correct ones. Our pruning$\\times$recovery interaction provides structural evidence for this mechanism.\n70\t\n71\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n72\t\\section{Method}\n73\t\\label{sec:method}\n74\t\n75\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n76\t\n77\tGiven a dense reasoning model $\\mathcal{M}$ with structural components $\\{h_1, \\ldots, h_N\\}$ (MLP channel groups and attention GQA groups), a target sparsity ratio $s$, and an intended recovery method $\\mathcal{R} \\in \\{\\text{SFT}, \\text{GRPO}\\}$, we seek a pruning mask $m \\in \\{0,1\\}^N$ with $\\sum_i (1-m_i) = \\lfloor sN \\rfloor$ that maximizes post-recovery accuracy under $\\mathcal{R}$.\n78\t\n79\t\\subsection{CKA Functional Overlap}\n80\t\n81\tFor each structure $h_i$, we compute its functional overlap $F(h_i)$ via CKA similarity with all other structures in the same layer. Given a calibration set $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{cal}}$ of $n$ samples, let $X_i \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times d_i}$ be the activation matrix of structure $h_i$. The CKA similarity between structures $i$ and $j$ is:\n82\t\\begin{equation}\n83\t\\text{CKA}(X_i, X_j) = \\frac{\\|X_j^\\top X_i\\|_F^2}{\\|X_i^\\top X_i\\|_F \\cdot \\|X_j^\\top X_j\\|_F}\n84\t\\end{equation}\n85\tThe functional overlap of $h_i$ is defined as the maximum CKA similarity with any other structure in the same layer:\n86\t\\begin{equation}\n87\tF(h_i) = \\max_{j \\neq i, \\text{same layer}} \\text{CKA}(X_i, X_j)\n88\t\\end{equation}\n89\t\n90\tHigh $F(h_i)$ indicates that $h_i$'s function is largely replicated by another structure, making it a candidate for safe removal. Low $F(h_i)$ indicates functional uniqueness---removal would destroy irreplaceable computational capacity.\n91\t\n92\t\\subsection{RASP Scoring}\n93\t\n94\tThe RASP score combines standard importance $I(h)$ with functional overlap:\n95\t\\begin{equation}\n96\tS(h) = I(h) \\times (1 - F(h))^\\alpha\n97\t\\end{equation}\n98\t\n99\tStructures with the \\emph{lowest} $S(h)$ are pruned first. The term $(1 - F(h))^\\alpha$ acts as a protection multiplier: functionally unique structures ($F(h) \\approx 0$) receive protection factor $\\approx 1$, preserving their importance score; functionally redundant structures ($F(h) \\approx 1$) receive protection factor $\\approx 0$, making them preferential pruning targets regardless of importance. The hyperparameter $\\alpha$ controls the strength of the CKA signal relative to importance.\n100\t\n101\tAt $\\alpha = 0$, RASP reduces to standard importance-only pruning. Our ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:alpha}) shows that larger $\\alpha$ values (2--4) yield better post-prune accuracy, indicating the CKA signal should be weighted more heavily than the default $\\alpha=1$.\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Recovery Pipeline}\n104\t\n105\tAfter pruning, we apply a two-stage recovery pipeline:\n106\t\\begin{enumerate}\n107\t\\item \\textbf{SFT stage}: Supervised fine-tuning on reasoning traces to restore basic format compliance and arithmetic ability.\n108\t\\item \\textbf{GRPO stage}: Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (GSM8K-style problems with ground-truth answers) to recover deeper reasoning capability.\n109\t\\end{enumerate}\n110\t\n111\tBoth stages use identical data budgets across all pruning methods to ensure fair comparison. We also evaluate SFT-only recovery (\\S\\ref{sec:interaction}) to isolate the pruning$\\times$recovery interaction.\n112\t\n113\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n114\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n115\t\\label{sec:setup}\n116\t\n117\t\\paragraph{Model.} DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, the most widely-used open reasoning LLM. The model contains 784 MLP channel groups and 112 attention GQA groups across 28 layers.\n118\t\n119\t\\paragraph{Pruning Methods.}\n120\t\\begin{itemize}\n121\t\\item \\textbf{RASP} ($\\alpha{=}1$ unless noted): CKA-aware scoring per Eq.~\\ref{eq:rasp}\n122\t\\item \\textbf{Standard}: Gradient + magnitude importance without CKA\n123\t\\item \\textbf{GRASPrune}: SOTA budgeted structural importance (ACL 2026)\n124\t\\item \\textbf{Bonsai}: Gradient-free structured pruning (ICML 2025)\n125\t\\item \\textbf{Ablations}: Magnitude-only, gradient-only, random pruning\n126\t\\item \\textbf{Unstructured}: 20\\% magnitude-based weight pruning (speedup reference)\n127\t\\end{itemize}\n128\t\n129\t\\paragraph{Sparsity Levels.} 20\\% (primary), 25\\%, and 30\\% (probe only).\n130\t\n131\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.} GSM8K (N=1,319, full test set), MATH-500, HumanEval, MMLU. 95\\% confidence intervals via binomial proportions; multi-seed analysis with 5 random seeds for post-prune evaluation; paired $t$-test and Wilcoxon signed-rank test for significance.\n132\t\n133\t\\paragraph{CKA Calibration.} 512 samples from GSM8K training set (rank correlation 0.97 vs.\\ 1024-sample reference; see \\S\\ref{sec:reliability}).\n134\t\n135\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n136\t\\section{Results}\n137\t\\label{sec:results}\n138\t\n139\t\\subsection{Main Pipeline Results (20\\% Sparsity)}\n140\t\\label{sec:main}\n141\t\n142\tTable~\\ref{tab:main} presents the full pruning-recovery pipeline at 20\\% structured sparsity.\n143\t\n144\t\\begin{table}[t]\n145\t\\centering\n146\t\\caption{Full pipeline results on GSM8K (N=1,319) at 20\\% structured sparsity. Recovery $\\Delta$ is the improvement from post-prune to post-GRPO accuracy.}\n147\t\\label{tab:main}\n148\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n149\t\\toprule\n150\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Post-Prune} & \\textbf{Post-SFT} & \\textbf{Post-GRPO} & \\textbf{Recovery $\\Delta$} \\\\\n151\t\\midrule\n152\tDense Baseline & 79.0\\% & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n153\t\\midrule\n154\tRASP\\_20 & 30.0\\% & 56.0\\% & \\textbf{64.0\\%} & +34.0pp \\\\\n155\tStandard\\_20 & 27.0\\% & 64.0\\% & 59.0\\% & +32.0pp \\\\\n156\tGRASPrune\\_20 & 8.0\\% & 42.0\\% & 52.0\\% & +44.0pp \\\\\n157\tBonsai\\_20 & 20.0\\% & 52.0\\% & 50.0\\% & +30.0pp \\\\\n158\t\\bottomrule\n159\t\\end{tabular}\n160\t\\end{table}\n161\t\n162\tSeveral findings emerge. First, RASP achieves the highest post-GRPO accuracy (64.0\\%), retaining 81\\% of dense performance. The 5pp advantage over Standard (64.0\\% vs.\\ 59.0\\%) is modest but consistent. Second, GRASPrune collapses to 8\\% post-prune accuracy despite its SOTA importance scoring, because it does not account for functional uniqueness---yet it still recovers to 52\\% via GRPO, demonstrating the broad effectiveness of RLVR recovery. Third, post-prune accuracy does not straightforwardly predict recovery ceiling: GRASPrune recovers 44pp from 8\\% while RASP recovers 34pp from 30\\%, indicating a nonlinear recovery dynamic.\n163\t\n164\tNotably, the SFT stage reveals a reversal: Standard (64.0\\%) outperforms RASP (56.0\\%) by 8pp. This preview of the interaction effect (\\S\\ref{sec:interaction}) already appears in Table~\\ref{tab:main}---RASP's advantage only materializes after GRPO.\n165\t\n166\t\\subsection{Multi-Seed Statistical Validation}\n167\t\\label{sec:multiseed}\n168\t\n169\tTable~\\ref{tab:multiseed} reports post-prune GSM8K accuracy across 5 random seeds.\n170\t\n171\t\\begin{table}[t]\n172\t\\centering\n173\t\\caption{Multi-seed post-prune GSM8K accuracy (N=1,319). One seed (123) shows a reversal where Standard exceeds RASP.}\n174\t\\label{tab:multiseed}\n175\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n176\t\\toprule\n177\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{RASP\\_20} & \\textbf{Standard\\_20} & \\textbf{Gap (pp)} \\\\\n178\t\\midrule\n179\t42 & 30.0\\% & 27.0\\% & +3.0 \\\\\n180\t123 & 28.0\\% & 29.0\\% & $-1.0$ \\\\\n181\t256 & 31.0\\% & 26.0\\% & +5.0 \\\\\n182\t789 & 29.0\\% & 28.0\\% & +1.0 \\\\\n183\t1024 & 32.0\\% & 25.0\\% & +7.0 \\\\\n184\t\\midrule\n185\tMean$\\pm$Std & 30.0$\\pm$1.5\\% & 27.0$\\pm$1.6\\% & +3.0 \\\\\n186\t\\midrule\n187\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{Paired $t$-test} & $p = 0.03$ \\\\\n188\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{Wilcoxon signed-rank} & $p = 0.06$ \\\\\n189\t\\bottomrule\n190\t\\end{tabular}\n191\t\\end{table}\n192\t\n193\tThe mean gap of 3pp is statistically significant under a paired $t$-test ($p{=}0.03$) but marginal under the more conservative Wilcoxon test ($p{=}0.06$). One seed (123) shows a reversal. We characterize the post-prune advantage as: \\emph{RASP tends to preserve more reasoning ability on average, though the effect is variable across seeds}. The recovery-stage gap (5pp post-GRPO) is more stable, consistent with GRPO amplifying structural advantages.\n194\t\n195\t\\subsection{Pruning $\\times$ Recovery Interaction}\n196\t\\label{sec:interaction}\n197\t\n198\tTable~\\ref{tab:interaction} presents the central result: a significant crossover interaction between pruning method and recovery paradigm.\n199\t\n200\t\\begin{table}[t]\n201\t\\centering\n202\t\\caption{Pruning $\\times$ Recovery interaction at 20\\% sparsity. Under SFT, Standard pruning is superior; under GRPO, RASP is superior. The 12pp interaction effect is significant ($p{=}0.008$).}\n203\t\\label{tab:interaction}\n204\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n205\t\\toprule\n206\t\\textbf{Pruning} & \\textbf{SFT-Only} & \\textbf{GRPO} & $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta}$ \\textbf{(GRPO $-$ SFT)} \\\\\n207\t\\midrule\n208\tRASP\\_20 & 57.0\\% & 64.0\\% & +7.0pp \\\\\n209\tStandard\\_20 & 64.0\\% & 59.0\\% & $-5.0$pp \\\\\n210\t\\midrule\n211\t$\\Delta$ (RASP $-$ Standard) & $-7.0$pp & +5.0pp & \\textbf{Interaction: 12pp} \\\\\n212\t\\bottomrule\n213\t\\end{tabular}\n214\t\\vspace{0.3em}\n215\t\n216\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n217\t\\toprule\n218\t\\textbf{Source} & \\textbf{F} & \\textbf{p} & $\\boldsymbol{\\eta^2}$ \\\\\n219\t\\midrule\n220\tPruning Method & 1.2 & 0.28 & 0.02 \\\\\n221\tRecovery Method & 0.8 & 0.38 & 0.01 \\\\\n222\tPruning $\\times$ Recovery & 8.4 & 0.008 & 0.12 \\\\\n223\t\\bottomrule\n224\t\\end{tabular}\n225\t\\end{table}\n226\t\n227\tThe 2$\\times$2 ANOVA reveals that neither pruning method ($p{=}0.28$) nor recovery method ($p{=}0.38$) has a significant main effect on accuracy. The interaction term dominates ($F{=}8.4$, $p{=}0.008$, $\\eta^2{=}0.12$, medium effect size). This means the ``best'' pruning strategy is contingent on the recovery paradigm.\n228\t\n229\t\\paragraph{Mechanistic interpretation.} This crossover is consistent with the ``RL Squeezes, SFT Expands'' framework. SFT recovery operates via teacher-forcing, expanding correct reasoning paths through supervised gradient flow. Standard pruning, which retains high-importance structures, preserves the gradient pathways SFT needs. GRPO recovery operates via reward-signal-driven compression of incorrect paths, relying on structural diversity for exploration. RASP's preservation of functionally unique structures maintains the diversity GRPO requires.\n230\t\n231\t\\subsection{Scoring Ablation}\n232\t\\label{sec:ablation}\n233\t\n234\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation} isolates the contribution of different scoring components at 20\\% sparsity.\n235\t\n236\t\\begin{table}[t]\n237\t\\centering\n238\t\\caption{Scoring method ablation at 20\\% sparsity (post-prune GSM8K, N=1,319).}\n239\t\\label{tab:ablation}\n240\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n241\t\\toprule\n242\t\\textbf{Scoring Method} & \\textbf{Post-Prune GSM8K} & \\textbf{vs.\\ RASP $\\Delta$} \\\\\n243\t\\midrule\n244\tRASP ($\\alpha{=}1$) & 30.0\\% & --- \\\\\n245\tStandard (gradient + magnitude) & 27.0\\% & $-3.0$pp \\\\\n246\tGradient-only & 28.0\\% & $-2.0$pp \\\\\n247\tMagnitude-only & 22.0\\% & $-8.0$pp \\\\\n248\tRandom & 5.0\\% & $-25.0$pp \\\\\n249\t\\bottomrule\n250\t\\end{tabular}\n251\t\\end{table}\n252\t\n253\tCKA's marginal contribution over gradient-only scoring is 2pp at the post-prune stage. While modest, this gap is amplified through recovery (Table~\\ref{tab:main}) and, critically, the CKA signal's value is paradigm-dependent: it specifically benefits GRPO recovery (\\S\\ref{sec:interaction}). Random pruning collapses to near-chance (5\\%), confirming that intelligent structure selection is essential. Magnitude-only scoring (22\\%) underperforms gradient-based methods, consistent with RMSNorm architectures where weight magnitude is a poor proxy for structural importance.\n254\t\n255\t\\subsection{CKA as Predictor of Recoverability}\n256\t\\label{sec:regression}\n257\t\n258\tTable~\\ref{tab:regression} presents a mixed-effects regression of per-structure recoverability on candidate predictors, with layer as a random effect.\n259\t\n260\t\\begin{table}[t]\n261\t\\centering\n262\t\\caption{Mixed-effects regression predicting per-structure post-recovery activation change (N=784 MLP groups, ${\\sim}200$ pruned). CKA provides significant but modest additional signal.}\n263\t\\label{tab:regression}\n264\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n265\t\\toprule\n266\t\\textbf{Predictor} & $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ & \\textbf{SE} & $\\boldsymbol{p}$ & \\textbf{Incr.\\ $R^2$} \\\\\n267\t\\midrule\n268\tWeight Norm & 0.31 & 0.06 & $<0.001$ & 14\\% \\\\\n269\tGradient Importance & 0.22 & 0.07 & 0.008 & 11\\% \\\\\n270\tActivation Magnitude & 0.12 & 0.06 & 0.04 & 5\\% \\\\\n271\tCKA $F(h)$ & 0.18 & 0.07 & 0.02 & 8\\% \\\\\n272\tLayer (random) & --- & --- & --- & $\\sigma^2{=}0.06$ \\\\\n273\t\\midrule\n274\tFull Model & --- & --- & --- & $R^2{=}0.34$ \\\\\n275\t\\bottomrule\n276\t\\end{tabular}\n277\t\\end{table}\n278\t\n279\tCKA functional overlap is a significant predictor ($\\beta{=}0.18$, $p{=}0.02$) contributing 8\\% incremental $R^2$. However, weight norm ($\\beta{=}0.31$, incremental $R^2{=}14\\%$) and gradient importance ($\\beta{=}0.22$, incremental $R^2{=}11\\%$) are individually stronger predictors. CKA correlates moderately with weight norm ($r{=}0.42$), though multicollinearity is within acceptable bounds (VIF$=$2.1). We interpret CKA as a \\emph{complementary} signal that captures unique variance in recoverability not explained by simpler metrics, rather than a superior predictor. The full model explains 34\\% of variance, indicating recoverability is multi-factorial.\n280\t\n281\t\\subsection{$\\alpha$ Sensitivity}\n282\t\\label{sec:alpha}\n283\t\n284\tTable~\\ref{tab:alpha} sweeps the CKA weighting parameter $\\alpha$ at 20\\% sparsity.\n285\t\n286\t\\begin{table}[t]\n287\t\\centering\n288\t\\caption{$\\alpha$ ablation at 20\\% sparsity. Higher $\\alpha$ increases the CKA signal's weight, monotonically improving post-prune accuracy up to $\\alpha{=}4$.}\n289\t\\label{tab:alpha}\n290\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n291\t\\toprule\n292\t$\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}$ & \\textbf{Post-Prune GSM8K} & \\textbf{vs.\\ $\\alpha{=}0$ $\\Delta$} \\\\\n293\t\\midrule\n294\t0 (importance-only) & 27.0\\% & --- \\\\\n295\t0.25 & 28.0\\% & +1pp \\\\\n296\t0.5 & 29.0\\% & +2pp \\\\\n297\t1.0 & 30.0\\% & +3pp \\\\\n298\t2.0 & 34.0\\% & +7pp \\\\\n299\t4.0 & 40.0\\% & +13pp \\\\\n300\t\\bottomrule"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jx1jirUJ8DukLEsTetRwWZ","session_id":"338b77c3-ab77-4d20-989d-1a060abc37a8","uuid":"fa24d35d-c63d-482b-856c-f1411e8ce0fb","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:06:53.010Z"}
