{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VBHTebJaPWfwdQuXjzbxLK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GEFa5Wp1yX9QGAGiwhvFq5","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_17/projects/recovery_aware_struct_prune/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T140651/median/paper.tex","offset":300},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14526,"cache_read_input_tokens":55166,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14526,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jx1jirUJ8DukLEsTetRwWZ","session_id":"338b77c3-ab77-4d20-989d-1a060abc37a8","uuid":"c2f68739-87f5-47c7-bf08-e67186645c42"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GEFa5Wp1yX9QGAGiwhvFq5","type":"tool_result","content":"300\t\\bottomrule\n301\t\\end{tabular}\n302\t\\end{table}\n303\t\n304\tPost-prune accuracy increases monotonically with $\\alpha$, from 27\\% ($\\alpha{=}0$, equivalent to standard pruning) to 40\\% ($\\alpha{=}4$), a 13pp improvement. This suggests the default $\\alpha{=}1$ substantially underweights the CKA signal. At $\\alpha{=}4$, the gap between RASP and Standard expands from 3pp to 13pp, substantially strengthening the case for CKA-aware pruning.\n305\t\n306\tHowever, the optimal $\\alpha$ is seed-sensitive ($\\pm$3pp across 3 seeds at $\\alpha{=}4$), and higher $\\alpha$ values are tuned on post-prune accuracy which may not transfer to post-recovery performance. We recommend $\\alpha \\in [2, 4]$ as a practical range, with $\\alpha{=}2$ as a robust default. Adaptive $\\alpha$ selection is a natural direction for future work.\n307\t\n308\t\\subsection{Sparsity Scaling}\n309\t\\label{sec:sparsity}\n310\t\n311\tTables~\\ref{tab:sparsity25} and \\ref{tab:sparsity_trend} examine behavior at 25\\% and 30\\% sparsity.\n312\t\n313\t\\begin{table}[t]\n314\t\\centering\n315\t\\caption{25\\% sparsity full pipeline. The interaction effect persists: SFT favors Standard, GRPO favors RASP.}\n316\t\\label{tab:sparsity25}\n317\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n318\t\\toprule\n319\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Post-Prune} & \\textbf{Post-SFT} & \\textbf{Post-GRPO} \\\\\n320\t\\midrule\n321\tRASP\\_25 & 18.0\\% & 48.0\\% & 55.0\\% \\\\\n322\tStandard\\_25 & 14.0\\% & 55.0\\% & 48.0\\% \\\\\n323\t\\bottomrule\n324\t\\end{tabular}\n325\t\\end{table}\n326\t\n327\tAt 25\\% sparsity, the GRPO recovery gap widens to 7pp (55.0\\% vs.\\ 48.0\\%), and the interaction effect strengthens: SFT again favors Standard (55\\% vs.\\ 48\\%), while GRPO favors RASP. Overall recovery ceilings decrease (55\\% vs.\\ 64\\% at 20\\%), approaching the practical utility threshold.\n328\t\n329\t\\begin{table}[t]\n330\t\\centering\n331\t\\caption{Post-prune accuracy scaling with sparsity. RASP's absolute advantage (in pp) increases with sparsity, but both methods approach collapse at 30\\%.}\n332\t\\label{tab:sparsity_trend}\n333\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n334\t\\toprule\n335\t\\textbf{Sparsity} & \\textbf{RASP} & \\textbf{Standard} & \\textbf{Gap (pp)} \\\\\n336\t\\midrule\n337\t20\\% & 30.0\\% & 27.0\\% & 3 \\\\\n338\t25\\% & 18.0\\% & 14.0\\% & 4 \\\\\n339\t30\\% & 8.0\\% & 3.0\\% & 5 \\\\\n340\t\\bottomrule\n341\t\\end{tabular}\n342\t\\end{table}\n343\t\n344\tAt 30\\% sparsity (prune-only probe, no recovery), both methods approach collapse. RASP maintains basic generation (EOS rate 15\\%) while Standard nearly collapses (EOS rate 4\\%). The RASP advantage in pp terms grows with sparsity (3$\\rightarrow$4$\\rightarrow$5), but practical applicability is bounded to 20--25\\% for this model.\n345\t\n346\t\\subsection{Baseline Comparisons}\n347\t\\label{sec:baselines}\n348\t\n349\tTable~\\ref{tab:baselines} compares against external baselines and unstructured pruning.\n350\t\n351\t\\begin{table}[t]\n352\t\\centering\n353\t\\caption{Comparison with external baselines and unstructured pruning at 20\\% sparsity.}\n354\t\\label{tab:baselines}\n355\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n356\t\\toprule\n357\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Type} & \\textbf{Post-Prune} & \\textbf{Post-GRPO} & \\textbf{Speedup} \\\\\n358\t\\midrule\n359\tRASP\\_20 & Structured & 30.0\\% & 64.0\\% & 1.20$\\times$ \\\\\n360\tStandard\\_20 & Structured & 27.0\\% & 59.0\\% & 1.21$\\times$ \\\\\n361\tGRASPrune\\_20 & Structured & 8.0\\% & 52.0\\% & --- \\\\\n362\tBonsai\\_20 & Structured & 20.0\\% & 50.0\\% & --- \\\\\n363\tMagnitude\\_20 & Unstructured & 68.0\\% & 62.0\\% & 1.00$\\times$ \\\\\n364\t\\bottomrule\n365\t\\end{tabular}\n366\t\\end{table}\n367\t\n368\tUnstructured pruning retains far more post-prune accuracy (68\\% vs.\\ 30\\%) because individual weight zeroing is less disruptive than removing entire channel groups. However, unstructured pruning achieves no wall-clock speedup on standard hardware (1.00$\\times$). RASP+GRPO (64\\%) slightly exceeds unstructured+GRPO (62\\%) while delivering 1.20$\\times$ speedup---demonstrating that structured pruning with CKA-aware selection and RLVR recovery can match unstructured pruning accuracy while providing practical efficiency gains.\n369\t\n370\tGRASPrune's low post-prune accuracy (8\\%) despite SOTA importance scoring illustrates that importance-only optimization without functional overlap awareness can remove structures critical for GRPO recovery. Its eventual recovery to 52\\% (vs.\\ RASP's 64\\%) shows that the pruning decision permanently constrains the recovery ceiling.\n371\t\n372\t\\subsection{Out-of-Distribution Evaluation}\n373\t\\label{sec:ood}\n374\t\n375\tTable~\\ref{tab:ood} evaluates whether RASP's advantage generalizes beyond GSM8K.\n376\t\n377\t\\begin{table}[t]\n378\t\\centering\n379\t\\caption{Out-of-distribution evaluation of GRPO-recovered models. RASP's advantage is specific to mathematical reasoning (GSM8K, MATH-500).}\n380\t\\label{tab:ood}\n381\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n382\t\\toprule\n383\t\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{Dense} & \\textbf{RASP+GRPO} & \\textbf{Std+GRPO} & $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta}$ \\textbf{(pp)} \\\\\n384\t\\midrule\n385\tGSM8K & 79.0\\% & 64.0\\% & 59.0\\% & +5 \\\\\n386\tMATH-500 & 52.0\\% & 38.0\\% & 33.0\\% & +5 \\\\\n387\tHumanEval & 58.0\\% & 42.0\\% & 41.0\\% & +1 \\\\\n388\tMMLU & 62.0\\% & 55.0\\% & 54.0\\% & +1 \\\\\n389\t\\bottomrule\n390\t\\end{tabular}\n391\t\\end{table}\n392\t\n393\tRASP's advantage extends to MATH-500 (5pp), which shares the mathematical reasoning domain with GSM8K. On HumanEval (code generation) and MMLU (knowledge), the difference is negligible (1pp each). This domain specificity is consistent with our mechanistic story: CKA-based functional overlap captures redundancy patterns specific to mathematical reasoning structures, not general capabilities.\n394\t\n395\tTable~\\ref{tab:sft_ood} further validates that the interaction effect holds on OOD benchmarks.\n396\t\n397\t\\begin{table}[t]\n398\t\\centering\n399\t\\caption{SFT vs.\\ GRPO recovery comparison across benchmarks. GRPO recovery delivers higher OOD retention, especially on MATH-500.}\n400\t\\label{tab:sft_ood}\n401\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n402\t\\toprule\n403\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{GSM8K} & \\textbf{MATH-500} & \\textbf{HumanEval} \\\\\n404\t\\midrule\n405\tDense & 79.0\\% & 52.0\\% & 58.0\\% \\\\\n406\tSFT-Standard & 64.0\\% & 30.0\\% & 35.0\\% \\\\\n407\tSFT-RASP & 57.0\\% & 28.0\\% & 33.0\\% \\\\\n408\tGRPO-RASP & 64.0\\% & 38.0\\% & 42.0\\% \\\\\n409\tGRPO-Standard & 59.0\\% & 33.0\\% & 41.0\\% \\\\\n410\t\\bottomrule\n411\t\\end{tabular}\n412\t\\end{table}\n413\t\n414\tOn MATH-500, GRPO-RASP (38\\%) exceeds the best SFT configuration (SFT-Standard, 30\\%) by 8pp, confirming that GRPO recovery delivers deeper, more generalizable reasoning recovery. The SFT ranking (Standard $>$ RASP) holds on MATH-500 (30\\% vs.\\ 28\\%), confirming the interaction effect is not GSM8K-specific.\n415\t\n416\t\\subsection{CKA Reliability}\n417\t\\label{sec:reliability}\n418\t\n419\tTable~\\ref{tab:reliability} validates that CKA scoring is stable across calibration sizes.\n420\t\n421\t\\begin{table}[t]\n422\t\\centering\n423\t\\caption{CKA scoring stability. 512 samples provide near-optimal rank preservation at moderate compute cost.}\n424\t\\label{tab:reliability}\n425\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n426\t\\toprule\n427\t\\textbf{Cal.\\ Size} & \\textbf{Rank Corr.\\ vs.\\ 1024} & \\textbf{Post-Prune Acc.} & \\textbf{Compute} \\\\\n428\t\\midrule\n429\t256 & 0.93 & 29.0\\% & 8 min \\\\\n430\t512 & 0.97 & 30.0\\% & 15 min \\\\\n431\t1024 & 1.00 (ref) & 30.0\\% & 28 min \\\\\n432\t\\bottomrule\n433\t\\end{tabular}\n434\t\\end{table}\n435\t\n436\tSplit-half Spearman $\\rho = 0.91$ confirms reproducibility. With 256 calibration samples, rank correlation with the 1024-sample reference is 0.93 and accuracy loss is only 1pp, making CKA computation practical (8 minutes on a single A6000). The marginal gain from 512 to 1024 samples is minimal ($\\rho$: 0.97$\\rightarrow$1.00), indicating diminishing returns.\n437\t\n438\t\\subsection{Inference Efficiency}\n439\t\\label{sec:efficiency}\n440\t\n441\t\\begin{table}[t]\n442\t\\centering\n443\t\\caption{Inference efficiency at 20\\% structured sparsity. Both pruned models achieve ${\\sim}$1.20$\\times$ speedup with negligible difference between pruning methods.}\n444\t\\label{tab:efficiency}\n445\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n446\t\\toprule\n447\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Latency (ms/tok)} & \\textbf{Speedup} & \\textbf{Throughput (tok/s)} \\\\\n448\t\\midrule\n449\tDense & 28.5 & 1.00$\\times$ & 35.1 \\\\\n450\tRASP\\_20 & 23.8 & 1.20$\\times$ & 42.0 \\\\\n451\tStandard\\_20 & 23.5 & 1.21$\\times$ & 42.6 \\\\\n452\t\\bottomrule\n453\t\\end{tabular}\n454\t\\end{table}\n455\t\n456\tBoth pruning methods achieve ${\\sim}$1.20$\\times$ speedup, with negligible difference between them (expected, since both remove the same number of structures). For reasoning models that generate 500--2,000 tokens per query, this translates to meaningful latency reduction. Speedup is slightly lower at batch size 8 (1.18$\\times$) due to increased compute-boundedness.\n457\t\n458\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n459\t\\section{Analysis and Discussion}\n460\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n461\t\n462\t\\paragraph{Why does the interaction occur?}\n463\tThe crossover between SFT and GRPO recovery preferences connects to the ``RL Squeezes, SFT Expands'' mechanism. SFT operates by teacher-forcing, reinforcing correct reasoning trajectories. This benefits from high-importance structures that maintain clean gradient pathways---exactly what Standard pruning preserves. GRPO operates by reward-signal-driven exploration, compressing incorrect paths while expanding successful ones. This benefits from structural diversity, particularly functionally unique components that maintain the exploration landscape---exactly what RASP protects.\n464\t\n465\t\\paragraph{Practical implications.}\n466\tThe interaction effect has a direct practical implication: practitioners should select their pruning strategy based on the intended recovery method. For SFT-only recovery pipelines (common in production settings with limited compute), standard importance-based pruning is preferable. For RLVR-based recovery (when maximizing reasoning quality), CKA-aware pruning should be preferred. The $\\alpha \\in [2, 4]$ range provides the best tradeoff, with $\\alpha{=}2$ as a robust default.\n467\t\n468\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n469\tOur study has several limitations. (1) We evaluate on a single model family (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B); generalization to other architectures requires validation. (2) At 20--25\\% sparsity, all pruned structures are MLP channel groups (0/112 attention GQA groups pruned); cross-component pruning dynamics require higher sparsity (${\\sim}$40\\%+). (3) Multi-seed validation covers the post-prune stage but not the full recovery pipeline due to compute constraints. (4) The $\\alpha$ sensitivity analysis reveals seed-dependent optimal values, limiting precise recommendations. (5) The full regression model explains only 34\\% of recoverability variance, indicating important unexplored factors.\n470\t\n471\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n472\t\\section{Conclusion}\n473\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n474\t\n475\tWe introduced RASP, a CKA-aware structured pruning method for reasoning LLMs, and identified a significant pruning$\\times$recovery interaction: SFT recovery favors standard importance-based pruning while GRPO recovery favors CKA-aware pruning, with a 12pp crossover effect ($p{=}0.008$). This interaction---not any individual pruning metric's superiority---is our central contribution. It demonstrates that structured pruning and recovery training should be co-designed, with the pruning strategy conditioned on the intended recovery paradigm.\n476\t\n477\tCKA functional overlap provides complementary predictive signal for per-structure recoverability (incremental $R^2{=}8\\%$), and the $\\alpha$ hyperparameter offers a practical lever to control the CKA signal's influence. The interaction effect persists across sparsity levels (20--25\\%) and extends to out-of-distribution mathematical reasoning. These findings motivate future work on adaptive $\\alpha$ selection, multi-model validation, and joint optimization of pruning and recovery objectives.\n478\t\n479\t%----------------------------------------------------------------------\n480\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n481\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n482\t\n483\t\\bibitem{deepseekr1}\n484\tDeepSeek-AI.\n485\t\\newblock DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing reasoning capability in LLMs via reinforcement learning.\n486\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2501.12948}, 2025.\n487\t\n488\t\\bibitem{selfhealing}\n489\tAnonymous.\n490\t\\newblock Self-healing pruned language models with RLVR.\n491\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n492\t\n493\t\\bibitem{graspune}\n494\tAnonymous.\n495\t\\newblock GRASPrune: Budgeted structural importance for LLM pruning.\n496\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ACL}, 2026.\n497\t\n498\t\\bibitem{resp}\n499\tAnonymous.\n500\t\\newblock RESP: Reasoning-aware structured pruning.\n501\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n502\t\n503\t\\bibitem{paser}\n504\tAnonymous.\n505\t\\newblock PASER: Optimizing recovery data for pruned language models.\n506\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n507\t\n508\t\\bibitem{mpruner}\n509\tAnonymous.\n510\t\\newblock MPruner: CKA-guided depth pruning for CNNs.\n511\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NeurIPS}, 2024.\n512\t\n513\t\\bibitem{rlsqueezes}\n514\tAnonymous.\n515\t\\newblock RL squeezes, SFT expands: Understanding RLVR training dynamics.\n516\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICLR}, 2026.\n517\t\n518\t\\bibitem{cka}\n519\tKornblith, S., Norouzi, M., Lee, H., and Hinton, G.\n520\t\\newblock Similarity of neural network representations revisited.\n521\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICML}, 2019.\n522\t\n523\t\\bibitem{bonsai}\n524\tAnonymous.\n525\t\\newblock Bonsai: Gradient-free structured pruning at scale.\n526\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICML}, 2025.\n527\t\n528\t\\bibitem{grpo}\n529\tShao, Z., et al.\n530\t\\newblock DeepSeekMath: Pushing the limits of mathematical reasoning in open language models.\n531\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.03300}, 2024.\n532\t\n533\t\\end{thebibliography}\n534\t\n535\t\\end{document}\n536\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jx1jirUJ8DukLEsTetRwWZ","session_id":"338b77c3-ab77-4d20-989d-1a060abc37a8","uuid":"6eb5a22a-1b73-4e30-acf6-af422201034b","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:06:58.596Z"}
